# dispositions.jsonl — the machine-readable index into ../intentional-differences.md.
#
# One JSON object per line. Blank lines and lines starting with `#` are skipped.
# The list is EMPTY, and it should stay short.
#
#   {"case_id":"…","field":"…","class":"intentional_difference",
#    "severity":"accepted","record":"../intentional-differences.md#<section>"}
#
# Fields:
#   case_id   the case the difference appears in. Never a wildcard: an exception
#             is granted to one observable in one case, not to a class of
#             comparison. A wildcard here is how a known exception becomes a
#             blind spot.
#   field     the comparator field exactly as it appears in a finding, e.g.
#             "process.exit_status" or "files.after[tasks.jsonl].sha256".
#   class     "intentional_difference" (Marcus accepted the Go behavior) or
#             "legacy_ruby_rule" (the Ruby behavior is wrong but is the
#             compatibility rule for this port; Go must still match it, so it
#             still fails the gate — the class records WHY, not a pass).
#   severity  "accepted" does not fail the gate; "gate" does. Optional;
#             defaults to "accepted".
#   record    REQUIRED. The section in ../intentional-differences.md that
#             records Marcus's decision. Loading refuses an entry without it —
#             ../intentional-differences.md § "The record" says a comparator
#             exception with no written record is a difference-hiding machine,
#             and this is that rule made mechanical.
#
# An entry here NEVER silences a finding. The difference is still reported, with
# its disposition attached; only its severity changes. Suppressing it outright
# would stop the comparison for that field, which is precisely how a blessed
# difference stops being watched.
#
# Only Marcus adds a line here. An agent that believes a difference is right
# files a blocked td issue naming him and moves on — see ../PORTING.md,
# "Never bless Go output".
